INT. PELLETIER FAMILY CABIN - NURSERY - NIGHT

A tiny room under the eaves. A white crib. A baby monitor on the dresser, its green light pulsing. Snow tapping the window.

ROXANNE PELLETIER, 30s, stands over the crib in a bathrobe. She holds the rail. GAVIN PELLETIER, 30s, hovers in the doorway, flashlight in hand. Their daughter MIMI, 4, clutches a stuffed rabbit and peers out from behind his leg.

She looks into the crib. It is empty. He looks at her. She looks at him. Mimi tugs his pajama sleeve.

GAVIN
Rox. Where's the baby?

ROXANNE
I don't know.

MIMI
Mommy, who's in the crib?

ROXANNE
Nobody, baby.

MIMI
The lady.

Roxanne turns. Mimi points at the crib. The mattress has a dent in it, the size of a small body. The monitor crackles.

MONITOR VOICE (V.O.)
(from the monitor, slow, lullaby cadence)
Hush now. Hush now. She's sleeping.

Gavin raises the flashlight. The beam shakes. He moves in front of Mimi. She grabs Roxanne's hand.

ROXANNE
Who is this?

MONITOR VOICE (V.O.)
Hush, hush. Don't wake her.

GAVIN
Where is she?

MONITOR VOICE (V.O.)
Under the bed. Under the bed. Under the bed.

Gavin stops. He looks at Roxanne. Roxanne looks down. A pale hand slides out across the floor, small and curled, holding the rabbit's ear. Mimi screams. The rabbit in her arms is gone.

Gavin drops to his knees. Pulls back the bedskirt. Nothing. Under the bed is a flat, white, empty dark. She grabs him by the collar and pulls him away from it.

ROXANNE
Gavin, don't.

GAVIN
It's our baby.

ROXANNE
It's not.

The monitor goes quiet. The green light stops pulsing. Then, from the hallway, quite near, a tiny, perfectly ordinary cry.
